A||||9||tomcat06085||88 GT body appearance||||5||
Z||000000||tomcat06085||10-09-2001||06:46 PM||||I like the appearance of the 88 GT's and never really got too close to Fiero's before my son got me interested. Out of curiousity, did all the GT's look like the 88's and if not when was the major change. It seems to me from what I see and read, that they all have the same appearance to the casual viewer. Of course, I won't be a casual onlooker for long...just bought an 88 GT and will try to get it ready for autocrossing in the spring. Thanks for any imput...everybody on the forum has been very helpful to me in my search for this car. Thanks for the help.<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Help me go fast!||||reg||5
Z||000001||skitime||10-09-2001||06:53 PM||||The body panels of an 88 GT are the same as the 86 and 87.  Only the aero trim was body color instead of silver. The 88 GTs had black or gold lace wheels and not silver.  The front wheel of an 88 GT is 1 inch narrower than earlier years.  Now brakes and suspension are very different but that is not visible to the casual observer.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WampusCat||10-09-2001||06:56 PM||||'85 GT was a notchback, 86-88 pretty much look alike except that the 88's were monochromatic and the 86-87 were two tone. <A HREF="http://www.goldengatefieros.org/history.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.goldengatefieros.org/history.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||tomcat06085||10-09-2001||07:06 PM||||[/B][/QUOTE]<BR>The front wheel of an 88 GT is 1 inch narrower than earlier years.  Now brakes and suspension are very different but that is not visible to the casual observer.<BR>[/B][/QUOTE]<P>What was the rationale for smaller wheels up from...from watching local autocrossers, it seems they understeer and this would contrinute to that.<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Help me go fast!<p>[This message has been edited by tomcat06085 (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2001||07:18 PM||||All the suspension components changed for the 88 model year. They finally get it right and stop production. Go figure!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake_Dragon||10-09-2001||07:32 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon14.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon14.gif">  <P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="red"><B>1985 GT</B></FONT><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gt.GIF">  <p>[This message has been edited by Jake_Dragon (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000006||skitime||10-09-2001||07:32 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tomcat06085:<BR><B>What was the rationale for smaller wheels up from...from watching local autocrossers, it seems they understeer and this would contribute to that.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am sure someone will correct me if I am wrong.  The new front suspension moved the front wheels further apart so the wider wheels were not needed to maintain wheel spacing and also the narrower wheel allow less steering effort.<P><p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wolfhound||10-09-2001||08:31 PM||||The 88 also has a smaller turning radius. The difference in off set allowed this without interference with the wheel well. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Flamberge||10-10-2001||01:01 AM||||I have yet to drive my current Fiero on ice and snow, but while living in Anchorage I cut my winter-driving teeth in my old 86 SE. <P>In Anchorage once the first real snows come, it never melts until April ("break up," as it is known.) So it just gets compacted down, with new snow on top of that. It is probably this way in other cold places. I never lost control (accidentally &gt; <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> while in the Fiero, but it definately suffered from understeer. I attribute it to the light weight of the front end. You turn your wheels, nothing happens until they finally grab. I should add that I never used studded tires, and one of the winters never even changed the high performance tires to the P195s. Not a bad winter car, considering.<P>- Flamberge||||reg||1
Z||000009||lou||10-10-2001||01:12 AM||||narrower front wheels on the 88 gt were for easier steering when stopped, nothing else. power assist was on the way in 89  but then.<p>[This message has been edited by lou (edited 10-10-2001).]||||reg||1
